1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,215
Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes. I
watched the newest one being born.

2
00:00:05,585 --> 00:00:08,575
It took four days. This is
how it happened.

3
00:00:09,445 --> 00:00:13,385
Lui, Goldie and Spot get a pinch
each, not the whole tin.

4
00:00:13,955 --> 00:00:18,545
Mum studied volcanoes. Dad
drove the number 72 bus.

5
00:00:18,915 --> 00:00:22,880
Lui had two goldfish in the
pond. And I had a notebook.

6
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,740
I was twelve.

7
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:29,115
Every Monday, my class tested the
creek behind our school.

8
00:00:29,485 --> 00:00:32,020
Fourteen degrees. Same as always.

9
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:34,930
Fourteen again? You and
your nerd notebook.

10
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,135
Nerds notice things.

11
00:00:37,805 --> 00:00:40,495
Everyone, step away from the trees!

12
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:44,805
By lunchtime, every phone in the
city showed the same alert.

13
00:00:45,175 --> 00:00:50,940
Ten kilometres under the city, hot melted
rock was rising, cracking its way up.

14
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:54,425
Auckland is built on about
fifty volcanoes.

15
00:00:54,795 --> 00:00:58,860
Almost all of them erupted just
once, each in a new place.

16
00:00:59,230 --> 00:01:02,520
So nobody could say where the
next one would come up.

17
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:05,180
Mum didn't come home for dinner.

18
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:08,315
She was at the monitoring
centre all night.

19
00:01:08,685 --> 00:01:13,675
It's about ten kilometres down, and it's
rising. It could take a few days.

20
00:01:14,245 --> 00:01:15,855
Where will it come out?

21
00:01:16,425 --> 00:01:19,640
We don't know yet. That's what
we're trying to work out.

22
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:21,825
A city is noisy.

23
00:01:22,195 --> 00:01:27,521
Trucks and trains blur tiny earthquakes, so
the scientists could only roughly tell

24
00:01:27,521 --> 00:01:28,560
where they were.

25
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:31,815
Is the volcano going to
come up under my bed?

26
00:01:32,185 --> 00:01:35,270
No. The city is huge, and
your bed is tiny.

27
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,105
Drop, cover, hold! Under the
table, both of you!

28
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:45,090
Just in case. Water, torch, radio, and the
first-aid kit. It lives by the door now.

29
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:47,595
It's fizzing. Like lemonade.

30
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:52,155
Nineteen degrees. Overnight, the
creek had warmed up by five.

31
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:58,990
Nearly every volcano in Auckland is a one-off.
It erupts once, and then never again.

32
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:05,025
So the next one would open somewhere brand
new: under a road, a field, or a house.

33
00:02:05,395 --> 00:02:10,185
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say their best guess is
under the harbour, but they can't be sure yet.

34
00:02:10,555 --> 00:02:12,915
Ana! Goldie and Spot aren't moving!

35
00:02:13,285 --> 00:02:17,668
They were fine this morning. And the pond's
at the very bottom of the garden.

36
00:02:18,038 --> 00:02:22,553
On his last run, the back of Dad’s bus
scraped on a bump in the road.

37
00:02:22,923 --> 00:02:25,163
It hadn’t been there last week.

38
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:29,798
There's a new bump on Kōwhai Street.
My bus scraped right over it.

39
00:02:30,168 --> 00:02:32,928
Kōwhai Street? That's right
next to the creek.

40
00:02:33,298 --> 00:02:35,288
I put all three on a map.

41
00:02:35,658 --> 00:02:38,498
They were all within a
kilometre of our house.

42
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:43,033
Before magma reaches the surface,
gas leaks out ahead of it.

43
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:48,043
That gas is heavier than air, so it
sinks into hollows and ponds.

44
00:02:48,413 --> 00:02:52,528
And as the magma pushes upward,
the ground above it rises.

45
00:02:52,898 --> 00:02:56,433
Dad, can you drive me to the creek?
I want to check it again.

46
00:02:56,803 --> 00:02:59,018
Get your coat. I'll get the torch.

47
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:02,598
Twenty-one degrees. It's
still getting warmer.

48
00:03:02,968 --> 00:03:06,328
Dad, feel this. Warm air is
coming out of the crack.

49
00:03:06,698 --> 00:03:08,413
Call your mother. Now.

50
00:03:09,083 --> 00:03:11,918
Mum, I think I know where
it’s going to come up.

51
00:03:12,288 --> 00:03:16,153
Ana, love, I'm really busy. Can
it wait until tomorrow?

52
00:03:16,523 --> 00:03:20,008
No. Our creek is fizzing, and
it’s five degrees warmer.

53
00:03:20,378 --> 00:03:24,838
Lui's fish died in the hollow, and the
road on Kōwhai Street is rising.

54
00:03:25,208 --> 00:03:27,473
Say that again. Slowly.

55
00:03:28,443 --> 00:03:32,853
The nearest GPS station has risen four
centimetres since this morning.

56
00:03:33,223 --> 00:03:38,213
We have a handful of instruments for a whole
city. She just gave us three more.

57
00:03:38,883 --> 00:03:42,548
Send the gas team to Kōwhai
Street. Right now.

58
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:48,058
Just before midnight, the gas team's meters
started beeping on Kōwhai Street.

59
00:03:48,428 --> 00:03:52,618
Ana? The gas team found it. You
were right. Pack a bag, love.

60
00:03:53,388 --> 00:03:56,953
On Wednesday morning, the
evacuation zone moved.

61
00:03:57,323 --> 00:03:59,463
Our street was right in the middle.

62
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:02,573
Every phone in the zone told us to leave.

63
00:04:02,943 --> 00:04:06,183
Twenty minutes. Take the
things you can’t replace.

64
00:04:06,553 --> 00:04:09,843
I took a photo of the four of
us, and my notebook.

65
00:04:10,213 --> 00:04:14,273
Mrs. Kaur, don't go down there! The
gas sinks into low places.

66
00:04:14,643 --> 00:04:17,983
I shook a box of cat biscuits
at the hatch instead.

67
00:04:18,353 --> 00:04:20,743
Cats always come for biscuits.

68
00:04:21,113 --> 00:04:23,678
Dad lowered the ramp for Mr. Tane.

69
00:04:24,048 --> 00:04:26,788
Nobody on our street was left behind.

70
00:04:27,158 --> 00:04:30,648
On you get, Mrs. Kaur. Your
cat rides free today.

71
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:33,783
I looked back at our house
from the bus door.

72
00:04:34,753 --> 00:04:38,093
Every lane of the motorway
ran one way: out.

73
00:04:38,463 --> 00:04:42,178
Forty thousand people left their
homes in a day and a half.

74
00:04:42,548 --> 00:04:47,413
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say a tip from a member of
the public helped them find the right place.

75
00:04:47,783 --> 00:04:50,243
That's you! You're a member of the public!

76
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:52,398
Will our house be okay?

77
00:04:52,768 --> 00:04:55,101
I don't know, Lui. But we will be.

78
00:04:55,871 --> 00:04:59,511
I'm going back for another load.
Look after your brother.

79
00:04:59,881 --> 00:05:04,316
Mum, what if I'm wrong? Forty thousand
people left because of my map.

80
00:05:04,686 --> 00:05:09,601
Then they go home on Friday, a bit
grumpy. Being wrong that way is fine.

81
00:05:09,971 --> 00:05:14,761
On Thursday afternoon, a police drone
flew over our empty street.

82
00:05:15,131 --> 00:05:17,621
The duck pond in the park was steaming.

83
00:05:17,991 --> 00:05:22,506
It's less than a kilometre down now.
Keep everyone out of the zone.

84
00:05:22,876 --> 00:05:26,741
At the hall, I wrote down the
depth from the TV every hour.

85
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:29,526
The numbers kept getting smaller.

86
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:34,961
NEWS ANCHOR: The zone is empty, and the power
there is off. Do not go back for any reason.

87
00:05:35,331 --> 00:05:38,821
Everyone in the zone is out.
Your map did that, Ana.

88
00:05:39,591 --> 00:05:42,601
Lui found the fish. And
Dad found the bump.

89
00:05:42,971 --> 00:05:47,336
Six hundred years ago, people here
watched Rangitoto erupt.

90
00:05:47,706 --> 00:05:50,996
Their footprints are still
there, in the hardened ash.

91
00:05:51,366 --> 00:05:55,381
Grown-ups, children, and dogs. They
didn't run. They walked away.

92
00:05:55,751 --> 00:05:58,491
Rangitoto rose straight out of the sea.

93
00:05:58,861 --> 00:06:03,501
Today it is covered in forest, and
families walk up it on Sundays.

94
00:06:03,871 --> 00:06:05,836
That night, I couldn’t sleep.

95
00:06:07,071 --> 00:06:09,986
At eight minutes to
midnight, the ground opened.

96
00:06:10,756 --> 00:06:14,621
I counted the seconds, the way Dad
taught us to with lightning.

97
00:06:14,991 --> 00:06:16,656
One, two, three…

98
00:06:17,626 --> 00:06:20,416
At twenty-three, the boom hit the windows.

99
00:06:20,786 --> 00:06:24,651
Sound takes twenty-three seconds
to travel eight kilometres.

100
00:06:25,021 --> 00:06:28,461
A volunteer read out every
name from our street.

101
00:06:28,831 --> 00:06:31,271
Every single person answered.

102
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:37,119
Where magma meets groundwater, it explodes
into steam, and a cloud of ash and rock

103
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:39,056
rushes out across the ground.

104
00:06:39,426 --> 00:06:41,791
Everyone it reached had already left.

105
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:44,346
Is that the park near our house?

106
00:06:44,716 --> 00:06:47,556
Yes. It's right at the end of our street.

107
00:06:48,726 --> 00:06:53,041
By morning, ash covered half the
city, and the airport was closed.

108
00:06:53,411 --> 00:06:58,351
NEWS ANCHOR: About two hundred homes were
destroyed. Nobody inside the zone was hurt.

109
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:00,536
Ours was one of them.

110
00:07:01,706 --> 00:07:05,446
We lost a house. Look around
you. We didn’t lose anyone.

111
00:07:05,816 --> 00:07:10,431
For two months, it fountained lava every
night and built itself a hill.

112
00:07:10,801 --> 00:07:12,591
Then it stopped, for good.

113
00:07:12,961 --> 00:07:15,801
When it was safe, Mum took
me to the fence.

114
00:07:16,171 --> 00:07:21,511
Our street was under three metres of ash. I
could see the top of our letterbox.

115
00:07:21,881 --> 00:07:25,121
We moved into a house on the
other side of town.

116
00:07:25,491 --> 00:07:27,606
I hung our photo up first.

117
00:07:27,976 --> 00:07:31,866
Lui got two new goldfish. He
named them Gas and Bump.

118
00:07:32,236 --> 00:07:37,925
Now schools all over the city test their
creeks every week, and send the numbers to

119
00:07:37,925 --> 00:07:39,501
the volcano scientists.

120
00:07:39,871 --> 00:07:44,136
A year later, the new hill had grass
on it, and a path to the top.

121
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:48,171
Mana whenua, the people of this
land, gave it its name.

122
00:07:48,541 --> 00:07:51,356
Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes now.

123
00:07:51,726 --> 00:07:54,741
The newest one is where my
bedroom used to be.

124
00:07:56,111 --> 00:08:00,301
I study volcanoes now, like Mum.
I still keep a notebook.

125
00:08:01,471 --> 00:08:06,211
If you had twenty minutes to leave, what
would you take? Tell us in the comments.
